{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at exact clean head 1be57f9ce08972a8d94f3ba9c053513ca5b61836, succeeding 8822a574. Companion to the internal/workroom artifact 0b80576b and the internal/app artifact 6eea808e at this same head; all three must be co-signed together. Answers request e8adc06c under promise 0fb763a0.\n\nFIVE PAGES, each saying the same new fact where its own reader will meet it. concepts/actors.md gains the rule under Custody, where membership is already explained: the genesis seed is the only state whose author need not already be live, every later state from a non-member is ineffective even when the repository still holds that principal's key, removal does not rewrite verdicts earned while admitted, and restoration gives force only to later states. concepts/work-loop.md now says the requester ratifies \"so long as that requester is still a live participant\", and that retiring the membership retires the authority with it — the later ratification is kept in the log but judged ineffective, even when the report was filed after they left. reference/architecture.md records it as an application-level boundary and says explicitly that these are fold rules, not kernel admission or signature rules: the kernel still accepts the signed record and the application decides what it means. reference/gs/ratify.md and reference/mcp/ratify.md both change \"the requester\" to \"the live requester\" in their authority tables.\n\nWHY THE ARCHITECTURE SENTENCE MATTERS MOST. The obvious place to look for this guard is the kernel, because it sounds like an admission rule. It is not one, and a reader who goes there will find the signature accepted and conclude nothing enforces membership. Saying which layer owns it is the difference between a reader finding the guard and reimplementing it.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED RATHER THAN COPIED. There is prior evidence for this work at unmerged head bbee4a4d, and its docs prose was a reasonable starting point. Two of its anchors no longer exist: work-loop.md has been rewritten since, so the sentences that diff was written against are gone, and architecture.md's section numbering has moved. I read the current text and wrote against it instead of applying the old patch, which would have either failed loudly or — worse — landed the right words in the wrong paragraph.\n\nCO-EXISTENCE WITH CODEX'S DOCS WORK, resolved before publishing rather than at merge. Codex held a live docs candidate at 74e84e64 re-anchoring README.md and anchoring.md. Our heads shared no file and merge-tree between them was clean, but artifacts key on the exact path string, so two live candidates at docs would still have been one path with two claimants and whichever merged first would have world-staled the other. I proposed the order in assert 31149ed6 — codex first, since theirs was docs-only and already published — held all three of my artifacts until 74e84e64 landed, then rebased onto 7a02f3e1 and published here. Nobody lost a re-cut.\n\nGATES at this exact head on a clean tree: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok, internal/docset among them and uncached, which is the gate that reads these pages and their citations; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0; go vet exit 0; go build exit 0; gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check clean. The rebase reverted nothing: three-dot and two-dot diffs against 7a02f3e1 are identical.","body":{"commit":"1be57f9ce08972a8d94f3ba9c053513ca5b61836","path":"docs"}}